{# THE CONFIGURE PAGE — the shell's, for every surface in the platform.

   ONE CONFIGURATION ENTRY, ONE PAGE. A module's settings, the words it lets a
   ranger pick from, the library its dashboard is composed in: all of it is
   behind the one `Configure` action and all of it is on this page, as sections.
   No module writes a settings screen of its own, and none writes a way back —
   the surface's first data tab and the crumb are the way back.

   THE DATA TABS ARE NOT SHOWN HERE. The section strip stands exactly where they
   stand, in the same component, so a person always reads one strip in one place
   and it always answers "which of these am I looking at". The frame renders it
   from `shell_tabs()` like any other strip; nothing below has to know.

   THE SECTION'S CONTENT IS THE SURFACE'S. Everything above the body belongs to
   the shell; the body is a template the surface named through the contract, and
   the shell neither reads a setting nor knows one exists. #}
{% extends '@Shell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ heading }} &middot; configure{% endblock %}

{% block shell_breadcrumbs %}
    <a href="{{ shell_brand().url }}">{{ shell_brand().name|lower }}</a> /
    {%- if back is not null %} <a href="{{ back }}">{{ heading|lower }}</a> / {% else %} {{ heading|lower }} / {% endif -%}
    configure
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}{{ heading }} &middot; configure{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_subtitle %}{{ summary }}{% endblock %}

{# THE SECTION, RENDERED BY WHOEVER OWNS IT. `only` is deliberate: a section
   template receives the section it is and nothing else out of this page's
   scope, so it cannot come to depend on a variable the shell happens to have
   in hand today. Everything it needs about its own surface it reads from its
   own services. #}
{% block shell_page %}{{ include(section.template, {section: section}, with_context: false) }}{% endblock %}
